At `eb0574d5528babd3b4aadece81590d8e2230b99b`, JavaScript async functions are preserved as carried source when targeting Rust. This blocks execution, retry-delay and failure-reporting adapters in [start#197](https://github.com/link-foundation/start/issues/197).

Minimal reproduction with Bun and installed `js/` dependencies:

```js
import { selfTranslate } from './js/src/self-translation.js';
const source = 'export async function execute(run) { return await run(); }\n';
const result = selfTranslate(source, 'JavaScript', 'Rust');
console.log(result.items.map(({term, status, reason}) => ({term, status, reason})));
console.log(result.code);
```

Actual: `export_statement` is `carried` with reason `unsupported`; output Rust contains only original JavaScript comments. Expected: supported typed async lowering, or an explicit diagnostic identifying asynchronous semantics and required callback signature/runtime contracts.

Workaround: keep native async/process adapters manually inventoried and check their behavior against common fixtures; generate only pure typed synchronous helpers. Original source preserved in comments must not count as generated executable behavior.

Suggested fix: recognize async/await in function declarations, require an explicit supported Future/callback signature, lower to native Rust async only when its runtime and error semantics are declared, and carry with an async-specific reason otherwise. Add executable async success/rejection/cancellation fixtures. #213 addresses signature inference generally; async syntax and scheduling/error semantics need separate support. Reproduction results are saved in [start#204](https://github.com/link-foundation/start/pull/204) under `docs/case-studies/issue-197/data/translation-probe.json`.
